1
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,956
A talkgroup is a channel that does not
exist — and it is the thing you actually

2
00:00:04,956 --> 00:00:07,563
listen to on a trunked system.

3
00:00:07,913 --> 00:00:10,839
On a trunked system, "Police Dispatch" is
not a frequency.

4
00:00:10,959 --> 00:00:14,570
It is a number — a talkgroup ID — naming a
group of users.

5
00:00:14,689 --> 00:00:18,741
When a member keys up, the system borrows
a physical channel for that one call.

6
00:00:18,861 --> 00:00:22,240
The next call from the same group may land
somewhere completely different.

7
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:26,410
Watch what that means over a minute of
traffic: call one lands on channel 3, call

8
00:00:26,410 --> 00:00:30,229
two on channel 7, call three on channel 2.

9
00:00:30,349 --> 00:00:32,218
The frequency changes every time.

10
00:00:32,338 --> 00:00:34,056
The talkgroup number never does.

11
00:00:34,177 --> 00:00:36,806
The group is the stable identity; the
frequency is a detail the system handles

12
00:00:36,806 --> 00:00:39,436
underneath.

13
00:00:39,786 --> 00:00:43,641
Every radio is programmed with the
talkgroups it can select and monitor.

14
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:47,986
Key up on one, and your radio sends a
request carrying that talkgroup ID.

15
00:00:48,106 --> 00:00:51,039
The control channel grants a voice channel
and announces the talkgroup, so every

16
00:00:51,039 --> 00:00:53,973
radio affiliated to the group retunes
together and hears the same call —

17
00:00:53,973 --> 00:00:56,906
wherever it landed.

18
00:00:57,257 --> 00:00:59,509
The IDs are numeric and system-specific.

19
00:00:59,629 --> 00:01:03,594
On P25 a talkgroup ID is a sixteen-bit
value scoped to the system's identity; DMR

20
00:01:03,594 --> 00:01:07,559
numbers its own way.

21
00:01:07,679 --> 00:01:12,040
Hobbyist databases publish alias tables
that map the raw numbers to human names —

22
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:16,402
police districts, fire, EMS, public works
— which is how a scanner display turns

23
00:01:16,402 --> 00:01:20,763
"talkgroup 101" into something readable.

24
00:01:21,113 --> 00:01:24,068
Alongside the talkgroup rides a second
number: the radio ID of the individual

25
00:01:24,068 --> 00:01:27,022
transmitting radio.

26
00:01:27,143 --> 00:01:30,394
Together they answer the two questions a
listener has about every call — which

27
00:01:30,394 --> 00:01:33,645
group is this, and which unit is talking.

28
00:01:33,764 --> 00:01:36,620
Group identity for the many; unit identity
for the one.

29
00:01:36,970 --> 00:01:38,479
Groups come in flavors.

30
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:41,718
Dispatch groups carry a desk's steady
traffic; tactical groups are short-lived

31
00:01:41,718 --> 00:01:44,835
channels stood up for an incident.

32
00:01:44,954 --> 00:01:49,040
A dispatcher can patch several talkgroups
together so they temporarily hear one

33
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:53,127
another, and an announcement group reaches
every subordinate group at once.

34
00:01:53,478 --> 00:01:56,612
This is why every serious trunking scanner
— GopherTrunk included — is organized

35
00:01:56,612 --> 00:01:59,747
around talkgroups, not frequencies.

36
00:01:59,867 --> 00:02:03,447
You build allow lists and priority lists
of groups; each active call shows its

37
00:02:03,447 --> 00:02:07,026
talkgroup ID, its alias, and the
transmitting radio ID; and the activity

38
00:02:07,026 --> 00:02:10,606
log counts traffic per group.

39
00:02:10,726 --> 00:02:12,944
The system hops frequencies constantly.

40
00:02:13,064 --> 00:02:14,202
You never have to care.

41
00:02:15,351 --> 00:02:20,146
So: a talkgroup is a virtual channel — a
number naming a group of users.

42
00:02:20,266 --> 00:02:24,138
Its calls hop across physical frequencies,
but the ID stays put, which is why you

43
00:02:24,138 --> 00:02:28,010
follow talkgroups and let the system worry
about frequencies.

44
00:02:28,130 --> 00:02:29,767
Full write-up linked below.
